{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\ncur=[i for i in json.load(open('state.json'))['interpretations'] if i['id'].startswith('5215af4e')][0]\nkn={k['id']:k for k in json.load(open('state.json'))['knowledge']}\nex=\\\"78769a572c5b4b249e441ac7ce63422a\\\"\n\npara=(\\\"Transfer-state probe, receipt %s (completed, tests_passed, exit 0): the same demote-then-transfer setup run twice with the leader's leadership-transfer flag sampled around each call. Parsed index 0 records the single-case state and parsed index 1 its result: the flag read false before the call, false as soon as the future was returned, false after the future completed and false three hundred milliseconds later; one attempt completed in 0 ms with no error, the named member never reported Leader across 283 samples over three seconds, no sample was leaderless, and the final leader is a different member. Parsed index 2 records the retry-case state and parsed index 3 its result: the flag read false before the first attempt, that attempt returned no error so the loop stopped after one, the named member never led across 284 samples, six samples were leaderless (about 60 ms), and the final leader is again a different member. Two things follow. The request was issued with no transfer already running and the library nevertheless accepted the demoted member as a target and reported the transfer as complete, so the earlier receipt's in-progress error is not evidence of a pre-existing transfer; and because the future succeeds while the named member never leads, the surface the caller sees is success, not a refusal, which is the form of the gap the account claims.\\\" % ex)\n\nlims=list(cur['limitations'])\nidx=[i for i,l in enumerate(lims) if l.startswith(\\\"Receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b does not establish\\\")][0]\nlims[i